<?php

namespace App\Services\Assistant;

/**
 * What the assistant knows about finding its way around the app: each area, the permission that
 * opens it (as in the sidebar), and how its common tasks are done. Only the areas a user can open
 * are given to the assistant, so it never explains screens they cannot reach.
 */
class AssistantKnowledge
{
    /**
     * [area, path, any of these permissions, what it is and how to use it, and optionally the steps
     * only some roles have: "permission|or another" => text, given only to users with one of them]
     */
    public const AREAS = [
        ['Dashboard', '/dashboard', ['can_view_dashboard_menu'],
            'Overview of sales, receipts, stock and alerts for the company, for the period chosen top right (default Last 30 Days). Cards: Sales = order totals incl. VAT by order date, excluding cancelled; Orders = orders dated in the period; Customers = all customers and how many were added; Net Cash = completed receipts in the period minus approved/paid expenses in the period (salaries, stock bought as expenses, PAYE/NSSF etc. all count), so it goes negative when more is spent than collected. The period picker also has Custom Range: pick From and To dates, then Apply. Each card\'s % badge compares with the same-length period just before: green up = better, red down = worse (a Net Cash going from -170,936 to -952,648 shows red -457%).'],
        ['Customers (CRM)', '/customers', ['can_view_customers_menu'],
            'Customer list. Add one with "Add Customer" (business name, contacts, KRA PIN, region/county/town, payment method; credit customers fill the credit application). A customer is always shown by their business / company name; the contact person only appears where labelled Contact. Click a customer to open their profile: Orders, Invoices, Account (balance, open invoices, unapplied payments, pending cheques), Statement and Notes. The Statement\'s ageing lists each unpaid invoice on its own line (S/No, invoice, invoice date, due date, days past due) with its balance under <30, 30-60, 60-90 or >90 days by its age counted from the invoice date (an invoice 121 days old sits under >90). Days past due is shown separately: the days since its due date, 0 if not yet due (121 days old on 60-day terms is 61 days past due). Finance > Aging also ranges by age. An invoice moves along as it gets older and drops off once paid; with no unpaid invoices the statement has no ageing section at all. The statement PDF never cuts a line: it goes on one page if it stays readable, otherwise it breaks between lines and repeats the column headings. It is worked out as at the To date, after credit notes and receipts (oldest invoice first). Moving an order or invoice to another customer takes its credit notes and any receipt or cheque that paid only that invoice with it; a receipt that also paid other invoices stays with the old customer and goes on their oldest open invoices or their credit, so the moved invoice owes that part. Accountants can delete credit notes. Customers > Accounts lists credit accounts and limits; the GM, admins and accountants approve or reject an account or a credit limit change from the row\'s "..." menu. To delete a customer: the row\'s "..." menu > Delete Customer, then confirm (GM, admins and accountants; any customer). A customer who has any order or invoice cannot be deleted (orders and invoices are never deleted, from 24/11/2026); a customer with none can be, together with their credit notes, receipts, cheques, quotes and collection notes. It cannot be undone. A customer statement is on the profile\'s Statement button; choose the period, then Download (Excel/PDF/CSV) or Print. Ticking "Pending Bills Only" (first under Include) makes it just "1. Pending Bills": every bill not yet fully paid (bounced-cheque debits too) in the ageing layout - S/No, Invoice No., Invoice Date, Due Date, Days Past Due, <30 / 30-60 / 60-90 / >90 Days and Balance, with Total, % of Total and Total Outstanding - no transactions or ageing - easy for the customer to read; print or download it the same way.'],
        ['Quotes', '/sales/quotes', ['can_view_quotes_menu'],
            'Sales > Quotes. "New Quote": pick the customer - or "One-time customer" in the customer list for someone not in the system: type just their name, the payment period (cash, 7-90 days, printed as the payment terms) and pick the Sales Rep (sales team); the quote shows them as "One-time customer". "Convert to Order" on such a quote asks which customer in the system the order goes under (the quote is merged into that customer); register them under Customers first if they are new. Then "Add Product" under the lines (the same search as on orders: type a description, item no. or code like NSPD 001 - matching price codes first, then one line per item at its NSPV price; a full code adds the line at once). Each line\'s code box can be retyped to change the item or use another list\'s code; set the quantity (price can be negotiated per quote), then save. Open a quote to view/print its document; "Convert to Order" turns an accepted quote into an order. Product search on quotes and orders lists every product, priced or not: one with no price on any list shows "No price set - type it on the line", comes in at 0.00 and the price is typed on the line. Sizes stocked as separate products share their list item\'s code (e.g. NSPO 110 for the reinforced gowns S/L/Xl/Xxl): type the code, then pick the size.'],
        ['Orders', '/sales/orders', ['can_view_sales_menu'],
            'Sales > Orders. "New Order": choose customer, order date, payment type (cash/credit), add products and quantities (prices come from the customer\'s price list), save. A credit order is always allowed: if the customer has credit invoices past their credit days, the form shows them in amber ("Overdue: ...") as a reminder to follow up the payment, but the order still goes ahead. An order is approved into an invoice (numbered NSK/####). Approvers: the GM, a Director, the Warehouse Manager or an Accountant - an accountant never approves an order they raised (Accountant A raises, Accountant B approves), and whoever raised it is not sent the approval alert. From an order you can create the dispatch. From 25/11/2026 there are no new past orders: every order entered, whatever its date, is checked against stock and takes it like any other (changing an order\'s date never makes it a past sale). Only the orders already marked "Past sale" (brought across from the old system) keep not moving stock. Every issued credit note puts its goods back in stock, including one on a past sale (CN-0023 on NSK/0430 was put back on 25/11/2026: 9 packs into the syringe batch).'],
        ['Invoices', '/sales/invoices', ['can_view_sales_menu'],
            'Sales > Invoices. An invoice made from an order always bills exactly the order\'s items, quantities, prices and discount: on Edit Invoice those items are read-only, and they are changed by editing the order (the invoice updates to match). Only the dates, terms, payment type and notes are edited on the invoice itself. (On 19/11/2026 an invoice edit put NSK/0461 out of step with ORD-0489 - gloves at 0.00 under another product - and it was re-matched to the order on 20/11/2026.) Invoices are made from approved orders and numbered NSK/#### (from 02/11/2026 the seriesstarts at NSK/0432). Open one to see lines, payments and balance; "View Document" shows the printable invoice with its delivery note, the invoice\'s Dated date and, above it, Invoice Date - both the invoice date, which is the ORDER date: from 25/11/2026 every order invoice, past ones included, is dated on its order\'s date, because the invoice is a product of the order (NSK/0232: ordered 04/06/2026, so the invoice is dated 04/06/2026 even though it was delivered 12/06/2026), and its due date is that date plus the customer\'s Credit Period. The delivery note keeps its own date (the dispatch date), shown as Delivery Note Date. Changing an order\'s date re-dates its invoice; changing a dispatch date changes only the Delivery Note Date. (From 24/11 to 25/11/2026 invoices were briefly dated on the delivery note date; that was undone on 25/11.) Nobody can delete an invoice or an order, from 24/11/2026: there is no Delete button; a wrong order is cancelled or rejected (it stays on record with that status and its stock goes back) and a wrong invoice is cleared with a credit note (kept compact: totals and VAT are the last rows of the items table, the Scan me QR sits beside the payment details, and it shows no amount paid or balance - those are on the invoice screen and the customer statement; when printed, an invoice that would spill onto a second page is shrunk to fit one page if it stays readable, down to 80%, about 10 lines, and a longer invoice keeps its normal size but is shrunk slightly when that removes a nearly empty last page); row menu > Download gives Excel/PDF/CSV. Supplier and delivery invoices are not here: the Suppliers page has their tabs: "Delivery Invoices" (the transporters DI-##### invoices, paid with their Pay button or from Supplier Payments) and "Supplier Invoices" - what suppliers billed us (bills such as PUR-131, and their debit notes from the filter): Supplier Ref (the supplier own invoice number), Our Ref (the goods receipt it came from, RCPT-0016), date, supplier, total, paid, balance, status (unpaid / part paid / paid) and "Paid By", the supplier payments that paid each (e.g. PV-161). Search finds an invoice by number, supplier, description or the payment that paid it; click a row to see each payment and the amount it put on that invoice. Supplier invoices are made three ways: (1) goods received create one automatically (amount = quantity x buying price plus shipping/logistics and the PO VAT, supplier invoice no. = the receipt reference, due date from the supplier payment terms; it follows the receipt if the receipt is edited); (2) "Record Supplier Invoice" for bills that are not goods (services, rent): supplier, their invoice no., date, due date, amount, VAT, description and optional account code (blank = Inventory); (3) Template then Import (preview first) for many at once, including debit notes (Type = Debit Note). New ones are numbered on from PUR- (debit notes DN-) and can be paid from Supplier Payments. A supplier payment or cheque captured in error is taken back from the supplier Account Statement (open the supplier): on its payment line, Cancel (amber, keeps it on record as cancelled) or Delete (red, removes it); the invoices it paid owe again, its cheque is cancelled and its ledger entry goes (Accountant, or anyone who can delete invoices). Export gives Excel (same columns as the import, so it can be edited and imported back), PDF or CSV. An unpaid recorded invoice can be deleted from its details; one from goods received changes with its receipt. Every tab shows Total Value, Paid, Unpaid and Number of Invoices at the top, for what the list is showing now: search a transporter or supplier such as Imevo and the four figures become theirs, and filters count too; cancelled invoices are listed but not counted. Every tab pages 20 / 50 / 250 / All rows (Rows per page under the table) and exports Excel/PDF/CSV.'],
        ['Credit Notes', '/sales/credit-notes', ['can_view_sales_menu'],
            'Sales > Credit Notes. "New Credit Note": pick the invoice, the items/quantities returned, the Credit Note Date and, under Approvers, the 2 people who must approve it, chosen from the GM and the Accountants (two different people, never whoever is raising it). A credit note dated 12/11/2026 or later is credited only when both approve it (one dated earlier needs no approvers and is issued with Issue, as before): each gets a bell alert, opens the credit note and, under Approvals, writes a comment (e.g. "Products in good shape" or "2 cartons damaged") and clicks Approve or Reject. Until both approve it shows "Awaiting approval (1 of 2)" and nothing is credited; when the second approves it is credited at once. If either rejects it, it is not credited ("Rejected"): edit it or use "Send for Approval Again" (choose the 2 people); editing always sends it to both again. While it waits, "Change Approvers" swaps the people (e.g. an approver was promoted to another role or is away); someone no longer GM or Accountant cannot approve. The list always follows each person\'s current role; when fewer than 2 GM/Accountants other than the person raising it are available (e.g. one Accountant was promoted), that person may be one of the 2 approvers themselves if they are GM or Accountant (e.g. the remaining Accountant and the GM); with fewer than 2 in the roles at all, it says so. Being credited returns the goods to the batches they came from and reduces what the customer owes. An invoice with nothing left to pay shows "Credited" (not "Paid") when credit cleared any of it or a credit note was raised on it - including one already paid in full with money, whose credit then went to the customer\'s other invoices (e.g. NSK/0022, credited by CN-0011 onto NSK/0039, 0114, 0148, 0161); a draft or voided credit note leaves it "Paid". To print or download one credit note: in the list, Actions (...) > "Credit Note (Print / Download)", or open it and click "Credit Note (Print / Download)". It is laid out like the debit note: letterhead; our details with the buyer (Bill to: business name, address, PIN) under them; Credit Note No., Dated, Original Invoice No. & Date, Mode/Terms of Payment, Buyer\'s Order No. & Date, Other References (customer phone - contact), Dispatch Doc No., Prepared By, Dispatched through, Destination (these from the invoice it credits), Terms of Delivery, Reason for Credit; Sl No., Description of Goods with Batch and Expiry under the name (where the returned stock went into a batch, else the invoice\'s batches; credit notes issued before 25/11/2026 on past sales put no stock back, so have none), Quantity, Rate, per, Amount; Less Discount and VAT; Total; amount in words; payment details with the Scan me QR; declaration and Authorised Signatory. Print, or Download as PDF, Excel or CSV.'],
        ['Cheques', '/sales/cheques', ['can_view_sales_menu'],
            'Sales > Cheques: post-dated cheques received, their maturity, clearing or bouncing (a bounced cheque re-opens the invoices and adds a KES 2,000 charge). Tabs: "Cheques" and "Bounced Cheque Debit", which lists every bounced customer cheque with its debit from the earliest to date: S/No, Debit No. (DEB-001, DEB-002...; click it to open the document), date, customer, cheque no., bank, cheque amount, debit, paid, balance and status, with search, paging and Export (Excel/PDF/CSV). A debit has its own DEB series and takes no invoice number or NSK number, so it is not on the Filing page. It is not listed in Sales > Invoices, only on this tab. Otherwise it behaves as an invoice: it shows under its DEB number on the customer\'s Account and on their statement and ledger, and receipts pay it in date order with the invoices. A cheque is always accepted, however old the invoices it pays: the customer\'s PD cheque credit period is a recorded term and refuses nothing. It always pays the oldest invoice first, through to the newest.'],
        ['Receipts (customer payments)', '/payments', ['can_view_payments_menu'],
            'Receipts: money received from customers. "Receive Payment": customer, amount, method (cash, M-Pesa, bank, cheque), reference. A customer\'s payment always pays their oldest unpaid invoice first, through to the newest, and nobody picks the invoice or the split: "Record Payment" on any invoice, "Map Payment" and "Allocate to Invoices" all work the same way, and the message says where the money went. Anything left stays as credit on the customer\'s account. Money is never refused (a cheque does the same when approved). Only money taken while creating a sale (a cash sale, down payment or order deposit) stays on that sale. Several payments may quote the same reference (e.g. two receipts for one invoice no.). A product short on stock never stops a receipt: the stock there is taken and the payment goes through. If a payment can\'t be saved, a red message says why and what to do; if it gives a reference, quote it to support. If an invoice was paid more than its total, the extra counts as the customer\'s credit (the Account Balance names the invoice) and "Apply Credit to Invoices" moves it to the open invoices, oldest first, so the balance matches the ledger. The cards at the top: Total Received is completed receipts only (failed and pending never count as money received); Failed Payments shows the sum of failed receipts (e.g. bounced cheques) with how many there are. Cancelling or deleting a receipt takes its money off the invoices it paid, then the customer\'s other receipts are re-sorted so the oldest invoices stay paid first and the newest invoice is the one left owing (the message lists what moved). The Invoice # column lists every invoice a receipt paid, oldest first (e.g. NSK/0265, NSK/0330, NSK/0345), and search finds a receipt by any of them; "On account" means the money sits as customer credit on no invoice. The page has two tabs. "Receipts" (the list above) has a period picker beside the status filter - All time, This month, Last month, This year or Custom range (From / To) - and a line over the table with the amount Received and the number of completed receipts for what is shown; Export (Excel/PDF/CSV) downloads that list. "Collections" is all money collected from customers: completed receipts (cash, M-Pesa, bank and cheques that have matured and cleared) plus received post-dated cheques still in hand (pending: Not matured, or Matured, not cleared - not yet approved). It opens on This month and has the same period picker (Custom range for any dates); a cheque counts on the date it was received, a receipt on its payment date. Cards: Total Collections, Received, Cheques in Hand and Number, all following the search, the type filter (All / Received only / Cheques in hand only) and the period. Columns: S/No, Type, Reference, Customer, Invoice #, Method, Date, Maturity Date, Amount, Status, with a Total Collections row; Export gives Excel/PDF/CSV. Bounced and cancelled cheques and failed receipts are not collections. An approved cheque appears once, as its receipt.'],
        ['Dispatch', '/dispatch', ['can_view_inventory_menu'],
            'Dispatch: deliveries of orders and requisitions. Create a dispatch from an order, choose transporter/zone (the Dispatch list shows each sale by its NSK invoice number, or ORD until it is invoiced, and search finds either; delivery rates set the cost per carton; the Warehouse Manager or an Accountant adds, edits or deletes a rate on the Delivery Rates tab, and the GM, a Director or an Accountant approves or rejects it, but an accountant never approves a rate they raised or last edited (Accountant A raises, Accountant B approves); editing an approved rate sends it back for approval), approve it, then print the delivery note (two copies: Original and Duplicate). Delivery invoices from transporters are paid under Sales > Invoices > Delivery. A rejected dispatch can always be approved again: open it in Dispatch and click Approve again (anyone with the approve-dispatches permission). It goes back in its approval line with the rejected step pending again, takes the order stock again, and is approved straight away when the person clicking is the next approver; it cannot be reopened while the order has another live dispatch (approve or cancel that one) or when the order is cancelled.'],
        ['Requisitions', '/requisitions', ['can_view_requisitions_menu'],
            '"Create New Requisition", add products (out-of-stock items can be added too, marked "Out of stock"), submit for approval; an approved requisition can be dispatched. While it is still pending, Edit Requisition (actions menu) changes its items, quantities, notes and approver: anyone with Update Requisitions, the requisition\'s own approver, or any Accountant can edit it. Product stock should equal the sum of the product\'s batches; an hourly check (stock:sync-from-batches) logs any item where they differ, without changing anything. On 25/11/2026 15 items differed: 14 product figures were right and their batches were quietly corrected to match, and Plaster Of Paris 4" was set to its counted batch, 11,287 (it had shown 0). These corrections were made silently: no movement is recorded for them. Stock adjustments now move the batches and the product figure together (before, a decrease without a batch changed only the product).'],
        ['Products', '/inventory/products', ['can_view_inventory_menu'],
            'Inventory > Products. To add a product: click "Add Product", enter the item description, unit of measure, cost price, tax status (VAT 16%/zero rated/exempt), reorder level, and its prices per price list (NSPV, NSPH, NSPO, NSPD, each with its item code), add sizes if it comes in several, then save. Many at once: "Import" with the Excel template (Export gives the same sheet to edit and re-import). Stock is the total of the product\'s batches.'],
        ['Price Lists', '/inventory/price-lists', ['can_create_products'],
            'Inventory > Price Lists: choose a list (NSPV, NSPH, NSPO, NSPD or a new one), "Download current" to edit prices in Excel, upload it, Preview, then Import. Importing NSPV also sets each product\'s default price. A row finds its item by code; if the row\'s description names a different existing item exactly (e.g. NSPV 036 written as "Ceftriaxone 1gm Vial" while the code sat on the 500 mg item), the code moves to that item and the preview says from where. Price lists never rename items: if the description matches no item, the price goes on the code\'s item with a note - rename the product itself (Products > edit). Each list takes only its own codes: an NSPO/NSPH/NSPD code in an NSPV upload (or any other list\'s code) is refused as an error row and nothing is saved for it; writing the list\'s own prefix is fine ("NSPV001" on NSPV is saved as 001). If a list already holds another list\'s codes (an NSPO sheet once uploaded as NSPV), a red box on that list says how many - "Show them" lists them, "Remove N misfiled prices" deletes only those (the other list\'s own prices are untouched), then upload the correct sheet.'],
        ['Batches', '/inventory/batches', ['can_view_inventory_menu'],
            'Inventory > Batches: each batch\'s number, expiry, quantity received/available and cost. Stock comes from batches; goods come in through Product Receipts. Only batches with stock are listed and exported; a used-up batch is kept (for returns, recalls and sales history) but hidden - pick "Sold Out (used up)" in the status filter to see them. One item can have many batches with the same batch number (e.g. one number delivered 10 times). To capture batches in Excel: Export > Excel on the Batches page, keep every row, add a row per new batch (Item Description exactly as in the system, Batch Number, Quantity Received, Quantity Available, Received Date, Expiry Date, Unit Cost), then Import Batches, check the Preview and Import. A row with the same Item Description, Batch Number and Received Date as a batch already there updates that batch (the quantity typed replaces it, it is not added); a row with a new Received Date, or an extra row beyond those already there, is a new batch. To correct a wrong batch number, just type the right number over it on the exported row and import: the row keeps its Received Date and quantity, so the import renames that batch instead of making a second one (on 24/11/2026 a corrected number on Calcium Gluconate 1gm/10ml Injection made a duplicate batch and doubled its stock; that was merged back into one batch 2506201 and the import was fixed).'],
        ['Product Receipts (goods received)', '/product-receipt', ['can_view_product_receipt_menu'],
            'The list shows every receipt, numbered RCPT-0001, RCPT-0002, ... (newest first, Rows per page at the bottom). The search box finds a receipt by its number as shown (RCPT-0031 or just 0031), the supplier\'s invoice no., supplier, store, who received it or document type; several words must all match. The cards on top: Total Receipts, Total Quantity, Line Items and Total Value (quantity x buying price on every line plus shipping and logistics, before VAT). Clicking a Goods received document in Reports > Inventory (stock movements) opens that one receipt on its own, with "Show all receipts" to go back to the full list; a Dispatch there opens its delivery note. "Create Receipt": supplier, store, document type and reference (supplier invoice/delivery note no.), then each product with batch number, expiry, quantity and unit cost; import costs (duty, clearing, transport) are spread over the units. Receiving against a purchase order: open the PO and click "Receive goods". Saving a receipt also creates the supplier invoice (Suppliers > Supplier Invoices) at those buying prices, so a line with a buying price of 0 asks for confirmation first. Returning goods to the supplier: on the receipt\'s row, Actions (...) > "Debit Note (Return to Supplier)", or the "Debit Note" button in the receipt\'s details, or Suppliers > Debit Notes > "Create Debit Note" (pick the receipt): enter the quantity going back on each line (up to what was received and not yet returned, and what is left of that receipt\'s own batch - goods already sold cannot go back), the date, the reason and optionally the supplier\'s credit note no. It raises a supplier debit note numbered SDEB/0001, SDEB/0002, ...: the stock comes out of that receipt\'s batch (sent to eTIMS as a supplier return), the amount (buying price x quantity, plus the PO\'s VAT) comes off what is owed on that receipt\'s supplier invoice, and the books post DR the supplier (Payables) / CR Inventory (and Input VAT). It shows in Suppliers > Debit Notes (which lists only these SDEB debit notes, with From Receipt and Recorded By; the DN- ones brought across from Tally are not there but stay in the books, in supplier balances and under Supplier Invoices > Debit notes) and in Suppliers > Supplier Invoices (filter Debit notes), with its items (batch and expiry). Clicking it opens its details: "Debit Note (Print / Download)" opens (in the same window) the printed debit note laid out like the company\'s Tally debit note (letterhead; our name, address, contact and e-mail with the supplier (Debit to) name, address and PIN under it; Debit Note No., Dated, Original Invoice No. & Date (the supplier\'s invoice the goods came in on), Other References (supplier\'s credit note no.), Goods Received No., Prepared By, Reason for Return; Sl No., Description of Goods with Batch and Expiry under the name, Quantity (e.g. 100 PCS), Rate, per, Amount; Total; then, as on the sales invoice, Amount Chargeable in words and E. & O.E, the PAYMENT DETAILS box (Company\'s KRA PIN, M-Pesa paybill and account, bank details) with the Scan me QR code, the Declaration, for Nescopharm Africa Limited / Authorised Signatory) with Print and Download (PDF/Excel/CSV). A supplier\'s PIN prints from the KRA PIN field on the supplier. "Edit" (GM, Director and Accountant to start: permission "Edit Debit Notes" on Users > Roles) changes the date, reason, supplier\'s credit note no. and quantities; it keeps its SDEB number, its old quantities go back into stock and the new ones come out, and the receipt\'s invoice and the books follow. Deleting it puts the stock back and the supplier is owed again. A receipt with a debit note cannot have its items edited or be deleted until the debit note is deleted.'],
        ['Stock Counts', '/inventory/stock-counts', ['can_view_inventory_menu'],
            '"New Stock Count": choose the store and the products (all, a category or picked ones); a product with sizes is counted size by size. The count lists one line per batch holding stock (batch number, expiry, expected = what the batch holds), plus one line for a product or size with no batch. Type what is on the shelf for each line (0 for an empty batch). Stock on no line: "Found stock" - pick the product and size, type the batch number on the pack: if it is already one of that product\'s batches the count is added to that batch, otherwise a new batch is created (give its expiry; the manufacturing date is optional and must be before expiry). Each line has S/No, Batch, Mfg. Date and Expiry. Selling can go on while a count is open: when a line is counted (saved), its Expected becomes what the batch holds at that moment, and approving applies only the difference (counted minus expected) to what the batch holds at approval - so sales made after counting stay deducted and sales before counting are not shown as missing. Then Start, Complete and Approve: approving corrects the counted batches, creates the new ones, records each change as a movement, and the product\'s stock becomes the sum of its batches.'],
        ['Stock Adjustments', '/inventory/stock-adjustments', ['can_view_inventory_menu'],
            'Adjust a batch\'s quantity (damage, expiry, correction) with a reason; adjustments are approved before they change stock.'],
        ['Suppliers', '/suppliers', ['can_view_suppliers_menu'],
            'The Suppliers page has tabs: Suppliers, Delivery Invoices and Supplier Invoices. Who can send out a dispatch: once approved, the Warehouse Manager, the Accountant, the GM, Directors and admins can press Dispatch Order > Create Logistics (and later mark it in transit / delivered, and add a new delivery person from the form). Transporters are suppliers: a delivery rate\'s transporter is picked from the suppliers, and names that differ only by Ltd/Limited, case or dots ("Imevo Ltd" = "Imevo Limited") are one transporter, so every delivery invoice adds to that one supplier\'s account and statement. Deleting a delivery rate deletes only the rate; invoices and dispatches already raised on it are not changed. A delivery invoice raised wrongly (wrong transporter, rate or cartons) is removed with Suppliers > Delivery Invoices > ... > Delete & Re-dispatch (Accountant or admin; unpaid and not yet delivered only): the invoice and its logistics record go and the dispatch is back on Dispatches with Dispatch Order to be entered again; stock and the delivery note are untouched. The Delivery Invoices list shows Order (the NSK number), Customer and Zone (from the rate when the invoice has none; imported old invoices are linked to the rate charged at the same price per carton, e.g. KES 290 = Upcountry, KES 200 = Nairobi). Logistics > Delivery Note shows "Issued - View" (the note issued with the order/invoice) and, separately, whether the customer\'s stamped copy was uploaded. The Dispatches search finds a dispatch by customer business or contact name (e.g. "reale"), NSK/ORD/ODI number, product, notes or who created it, across all dispatches; the cards count everything the search and filters match. Delivery cost: when a dispatch is sent out (Dispatch Order > Delivery Cost) the Total Delivery Cost starts at the transporter\'s rate x cartons (e.g. 4 x KES 290 = KES 1,160) but can be overwritten with the amount agreed with the transporter for that dispatch (e.g. KES 1,000); that amount is the cost and the Delivery Invoice (DI-#####), and the invoice notes what the rate gave ("Use the rate" puts it back). Accounting can correct any delivery invoice afterwards: row menu > "Edit Amount / Details" (amount, cartons, transporter invoice no., date, notes; a paid one\'s payment follows, every amount change is noted with who and when), or many at once with "Download to Edit" (the invoices in the import layout, following the search) - change Amount etc. in Excel, keep each Invoice No., and Import it back (preview first). ' . '"Add Supplier" with contacts, payment terms, bank details and KRA PIN; Import/Template for many at once. A supplier\'s statement shows bills and payments, and under Owed splits what is "Unpaid, within N days" (still inside the supplier\'s credit terms, Payment Terms Days on the supplier) from "Overdue, over N days", with the next due date. Its "Full Statement" button opens /suppliers/{id}/statement, laid out like the customer statement: From/To, All Time and month/year presets, the supplier and an Account Summary (credit terms, opening balance, billed, payments & debit notes, amount owed, of which within terms / overdue), 1. Transaction Statement, 2. Bills Payable & Ageing (each open bill: due date = bill date + credit days, payments and debit notes clear the oldest bills first, Status Paid / Part Paid / Unpaid / Overdue, days overdue, aged Within terms (0-N days), N+1 to N+30, N+31 to N+60, over N+60), and Include ticks: Pending Bills Only, All Bills (paid too) and Payment Plan with a Pay by date (overdue now + falling due up to that date = total to pay in the batch, then what is due later, and a due-date schedule) - the same as the old Bills Payable Tracker Excel. Print, and Download as PDF, Excel (one sheet per section) or CSV. To pay what is due: on the supplier\'s Full Statement page, tick "Bills Due for Payment" in the Include row (a tick like Pending Bills Only; the page then shows just these instead of the statement) to see that supplier\'s unpaid bills and delivery invoices (it is only there, not on the Payments page), each due on its date + that supplier\'s credit days (e.g. Imevo Net 30: a bill 30 or more days old is due). Cards: Due now, Falling due by the Pay by date, Total to pay by that date, Not yet due, Cheques written not cleared (already taken off To Pay so nothing is paid twice). Show: Due now (default) / Due by the pay-by date / All unpaid; Export. Each bill shows Bill Date, Due Date, Reference, Balance, To Pay, Days Overdue, Status; tick bills (or the supplier row for all) and press Pay - the payment form opens on that supplier with those invoices ticked and the amount filled; choose method (cheque needs number, bank, maturity) and save (needs Create Payments). Its "Ledger (Double Entry)" box is the supplier\'s ledger account from the books in the Tally layout - Date, Dr/Cr, Particulars (the other side of the entry, e.g. "Cr Bank Accounts" for a payment, "Dr Inventory" for a purchase), Vch Type, Vch No., Debit, Credit - opening with the Opening Balance and ending with Total and the Closing Balance (Cr = we owe them, written in the Debit column as Tally does); choose From/To and Apply, "Show edits & reversals" to see corrected entries, Export for Excel/PDF/CSV. The "General Ledger" button on a supplier (or transporter) opens Finance > General Ledger filtered to them: every journal entry for their bills, delivery invoices, goods received, debit notes and payments, with both sides (e.g. Dr Inventory or Transport / Cr Accounts Payable for a bill; Dr Accounts Payable / Cr Bank for a payment); the Payables account closing balance is what is owed to them.'],
        ['Purchase Orders', '/purchase-orders', ['can_view_purchase_orders_menu'],
            '"New Purchase Order": supplier, store, order date, products with quantities and cost. It is approved ("Approve") by the GM, a Director or an Accountant - an accountant never approves a purchase order they raised (another accountant, the GM or a Director does), and the one who raised it is not sent the approval alert - then goods are received against it ("Receive goods" creates the product receipt). Row menu: View Details, Download, Return to supplier.'],
        ['Supplier Payments', '/supplier-payments', ['can_view_payments_menu'],
            '"Record Supplier Payment": supplier, amount, date, Paid from - Petty Cash / Cash at Hand (cash) or Prime Bank (then Paid by: Bank Transfer, Cheque or M-Pesa) - and reference (and the PO if it is for one). The ledger account is always the supplier\'s Accounts Payables (shown on the form), because the bill already carried the cost; the list shows Paid From (Petty Cash, or Prime Bank (Cheque) etc.). A transporter\'s delivery invoice Pay button has the same Paid from choice. Picking the supplier lists its unpaid invoices under "Invoices Paid" - its bills and, for a transporter, its delivery invoices - oldest first: tick each invoice this payment pays (it fills the full amount owed, which can be lowered for a part payment; the payment amount follows), or type the amount and click "Pay oldest first". One payment (e.g. one cheque) can pay many invoices. Cash, bank and M-Pesa pay the invoices at once; a cheque pays them only when it is marked cleared. The payments list shows each payment\'s invoices (Invoices / PO column, searchable), and View Details lists them with what was paid on each. The invoices themselves are listed under Sales > Invoices > Supplier Invoices. The list pages 20 / 50 / 250 / All rows.'],
        ['Expenses', '/expenses', ['can_view_expenses_menu'],
            '"Add Expense" form, in four steps: 1 Ledger Account - pick the main Account (any main account of the chart: Assets, Liabilities, Capital Account, Income, Cost of Sales, Expenses...) then the Sub-account under it (e.g. Transport under Expenses, Cost of Goods Sold under Cost of Sales); the expense is debited to exactly that account. Prime Bank and Cash at Hand are not offered - they are what pays (money moved between them is a transfer). 2 Payment - Paid from "Petty Cash / Cash at Hand" (cash) or "Prime Bank" (Paid by Cheque or Bank Transfer); the toggle "Amount includes VAT (16%)": on = the amount paid includes VAT and the form shows Amount paid, VAT and Expense (excl. VAT) - e.g. 232,000 holds VAT 32,000, so 200,000 goes to the expense account and 32,000 to the VAT Account (Liabilities > Duties & Taxes), while the full 232,000 comes out of Petty Cash / Prime Bank; off = the amount has no VAT and all of it is the expense. The expense details show the VAT, the export has VAT and Excl. VAT columns, and the import has an Includes VAT (Yes/No) column; Amount and Date. 3 Details - Paid To, Store, Description, Notes. 4 Receipt, recurring, tags (and Status when editing). The list shows S/No, Date, Description, Ledger Account, Paid From, Amount, Status; filter by ledger account (a whole main account or one sub-account), status, search. Export gives Excel/PDF/CSV with Account, Ledger Account and Paid From. Import menu: "Import from Excel" (preview first, then save), "Download blank template" (Date, Account, Sub-account, Paid From, Paid By, Amount, Paid To, Description, Store, Status, Notes - sub-account by code or name; Status blank = Approved), and "Download to Edit" - the expenses listed (after filters) with their Expense Ref, so the accountant can correct old expenses in bulk (e.g. move them to the right sub-account or change Paid From) and upload the file back: rows with an Expense Ref update that expense and its ledger entry is re-posted to the new account; rows without one are added; an identical expense already there is skipped. One expense can also be corrected with Edit. Old expenses were given the account their category posted to. Payment decides which book it comes out of: Cash = Cash at Hand / Petty Cash (Cash Book), Cheque or Bank Transfer = Prime Bank (Bank Book). Cash withdrawn from Prime Bank is NOT an expense: record it under Finance > Bank Accounts > Withdraw / Transfer (Prime Bank to Cash at Hand), then record what the cash paid for as cash expenses. An expense that was really a withdrawal: open it and press "Was a bank withdrawal" - the expense is removed and a transfer (TRF-...) for the same date and amount is recorded instead.'],
        ['Filing', '/filing', ['can_view_filing'],
            'Every invoice by customer with the File No its printed copy is filed under (NSK number). File numbers can be typed in or filled in the exported Excel and imported back.'],
        ['Finance', '/finance', ['can_view_finance_menu'],
            'Finance > Aging (receivables and payables): the Collection Summary lists each customer then each of their invoices, with Date (the invoice date), Promise Date and Collection Notes each in its own column (every table and export keeps dates in their own column). Receivables use every unpaid invoice in Sales > Invoices, whatever its status, plus unpaid bounced cheque debits (DEB-...), which Sales > Invoices does not list. Collection Summary shows each customer\'s unpaid invoices and debits under their line, and so does its export. Total Outstanding is everything unpaid, due or not, split into Not Yet Due and Overdue. The cards and totals follow the search, which finds a customer by any words of its name or contact, or an invoice number, on both Collection Summary and Invoice Tracker; the Invoice Tracker also shows each customer\'s total outstanding. The page refreshes by itself. The 0-30, 31-60, 61-90 and >90 day columns go by each invoice\'s age from its invoice date (121 days old sits in >90). Days Overdue, Not Yet Due and Overdue go by the due date, which is the invoice date plus the customer\'s Credit Period, whatever the invoice was saved with (121 days old on 60-day terms is 61 days overdue); changing a customer\'s Credit Period moves their invoices with it, a bounced cheque debit stays due on receipt, and a customer with no Credit Period keeps the invoice\'s own terms. Finance > Reports > Income Statement is the full profit and loss for a chosen period: Revenue to Net Revenue, Cost of Sales to Gross Profit, Operating Expenses (Selling & Marketing, General & Administrative, R&D, Non-Cash) to Operating Income / EBIT, Non-Operating items to Earnings Before Taxes, then Taxes to Net Profit. Each ledger account sits on a line by its name and is listed under it; one no line recognises goes on an Other line, so the statement always equals the ledger. Export gives Excel/PDF/CSV. Reports leave out corrections (an entry replaced when its document was edited or removed, and its reversal), so each month shows that month\'s invoices; a bounced cheque stays. Income Statement sales exclude VAT, while the Sales report\'s Sales Accounts show invoice totals including VAT and list only sales from orders, so bounced cheque charges appear only on the statement. An amber note warns when Cost of Sales looks too low or no income tax is recorded. Finance > Journal Entries: S/No, Entry #, Date, Description, Type, Debit, Credit, Status; row menu View / Edit / Post / Delete / Reverse. Drafts can be edited, posted or deleted. To clean up records brought over from the old system, anyone with "Edit Posted Journal Entries" (Accountant, GM, Director) can also Edit a posted entry in place (it stays posted, the change goes straight into the books, and the entry keeps a note of who changed it and what it was) or Delete a posted or reversed entry outright: deleting an entry that was reversed deletes its reversal too, and deleting only a reversal makes the original count as posted again. An entry posted automatically from a document (invoice, payment, expense...) can be edited the same way, but that does not change the document, and editing the document later posts it afresh. Chart of Accounts: Edit Account (the row\'s menu) changes every field - code, name, type, subtype (a list for the type; Cost of Sales accounts are type Expense, subtype Cost of Goods Sold), normal balance, currency, opening balance, current balance, active and description. Current balance = opening balance + what has been posted; type either one and the other follows (typing a current balance sets the opening balance to the difference; posted entries are not touched). Anyone with Delete Accounts (Accountant, GM, Director) can delete an account of their company, except a system account, one with sub-accounts, one with journal entry lines, or one used by a bank transfer - the error says which and how many; move or delete those first, or mark the account Inactive. Finance: Chart of Accounts, Journal Entries, General Ledger (per account, filter by date/account/customer/supplier or transporter - a customer or supplier filter shows only their entries with both sides of each; every account opens with an Opening Balance row and ends with a Closing Balance row with total debits and credits, and an account with a balance brought forward but no entries in the period still shows; Download), Trial Balance, Balance Sheet, Income Statement (profit and loss), Cash Flow, Financial Ratios, Aging, Bank & Cash Books (Prime Bank and Cash at Hand, each with a running balance), Bank Accounts (withdrawals/transfers), Budgets, Tax Rates, Account Mappings.'],
        ['Finance > Bank Reconciliation', '/finance/bank-reconciliation', ['can_view_bank_accounts', 'can_view_bank_reconciliation'],
            'Bank Reconciliation Statement (BRS), as in Tally, for Prime Bank. 1. Pick the bank card (it says "Reconciled to dd/mm/yyyy" or "Never reconciled"). 2. Set Statement date (as at) and type Balance as per bank statement, then Apply. 3. The table lists the Bank Book entries - Show "Not yet in bank" (entries up to the statement date with no bank date, plus later entries the bank already shows) or "All entries" (with an optional From date): S/No, Date, Particulars, Vch Type, Vch No., Deposits (Debit), Withdrawals (Credit), Bank Date, Status (In bank / Not in bank yet / In bank, later in books). Type each entry\'s Bank Date - the date it appears on the bank statement - and it saves as soon as you leave the box; or tick several and use "Set bank date" (the date typed), "Bank date = entry date", or "Clear bank date". 4. The panel on the right works it out: Balance as per Company Books, Less deposits not yet credited by the bank, Add payments not yet debited by the bank, Add/(Less) amounts in the bank not yet in the books = Balance as per Bank (from the books), against Balance as per Bank Statement; Difference turns green at 0.00. 5. "Save reconciliation to <date>" (only when the difference is 0.00; optional notes) records it and locks the bank dates of the entries it covered (lock icon). Saved reconciliations are listed below with statement and book balances, who reconciled and when; Reopen (latest only) unlocks them, keeping the bank dates. A new reconciliation must be dated after the last one. Anything on the statement but missing from the books is recorded on its own screen first (links on the page: bank charges as an expense on Expenses > Bank Charges paid from Prime Bank; withdrawals on Bank Accounts > Withdraw / Transfer; supplier payments; journal entries) and then given its bank date here. Export (Excel/PDF/CSV) gives the statement with the summary; the saved list exports too. Giving bank dates, saving and reopening need Update Bank Accounts (Accountant, GM, Director). The stand-in history withdrawals (TRF-0002 to TRF-0023, HIST-YYYY-MM) were deleted on 20/11/2026, and so was the test transfer TRF-0001: past cash withdrawals are not in the books until the real ones are recorded from the bank statement, so Cash at Hand shows minus about 17.76M and Prime Bank is higher than the bank until then.'],
        ['Finance > Bank & Cash Books', '/finance/cash-books', ['can_view_bank_accounts', 'can_view_general_ledger'],
            'Bank Book (Prime Bank) and Cash Book (Cash at Hand / Petty Cash), each its own book so the bank reconciles on its own and all cash withdrawn is accounted for. Pick the book from the cards (each shows its balance), set From/To, Apply. Tally layout: S/No, Date, Dr/Cr, Particulars (customer or supplier, or the expense account), Vch Type (Receipt, Payment, Contra = bank-to-cash transfer, Journal), Vch No., Receipts (Debit), Payments (Credit), running Balance with Dr/Cr; Opening Balance first, then Total and Closing Balance. "Show edits & reversals" shows corrected entries. Export gives Excel/PDF/CSV. Customer cheques, M-Pesa and bank transfers go into Prime Bank; supplier cheques come out of Prime Bank and reduce that supplier\'s account; cash expenses come out of Cash at Hand; a withdrawal (Finance > Bank Accounts > Withdraw / Transfer) is a payment in the Bank Book and a receipt in the Cash Book. Balance Sheet: Capital Account = the owners\' capital only, one line per director (NEDADUR RAVICHANDRAN and KIMUTAI BETHUEL CHESIR, 20,000,000 each, paid into Prime Bank on 01/01/2025); equity is the Capital Account alone - there is no Opening Balance Difference account (removed 24/11/2026), balances brought across from the old system go to the Capital Account; profit shows as Profit & Loss A/c (previous years / current year), never as other equity accounts. The books are for viewing and exporting only: they have NO "New" button and NO Payment/Receipt/Journal entry form. Reconciling with the bank statement is done on Finance > Bank Reconciliation (/finance/bank-reconciliation). Anything on the statement but missing from the book is entered on its own screen and then shows in the book: bank charges, bank fees and similar - Expenses > Add Expense with Account Expenses, Sub-account Bank Charges, Paid from Prime Bank, Paid by Bank Transfer (or, for the charge on a withdrawal, the Charges field of Finance > Bank Accounts > Withdraw / Transfer); a cash withdrawal - Finance > Bank Accounts > Withdraw / Transfer; money received from a customer - a receipt on the customer; a supplier payment - Supplier Payments; anything else - Finance > Journal Entries. The stand-in history withdrawals (TRF-0002 to TRF-0023, HIST-YYYY-MM) were deleted on 20/11/2026, and so was the test transfer TRF-0001: past cash withdrawals are not in the books until the real ones are recorded from the bank statement, so Cash at Hand shows minus about 17.76M and Prime Bank is higher than the bank until then.'],
        ['Sales Reports', '/reports/sales', ['can_view_reports_menu'],
            'Reports > Sales: pick the period, then tabs Sales Accounts (ledger, with "Show receipts"), Orders, Products, Customers, By Region, By County, By Town, By Sales Rep, Daily Log. Every table has Export (Excel/PDF/CSV).'],
        ['Inventory Reports', '/reports/inventory', ['can_view_reports_menu'],
            'Reports > Inventory: every item has a tick box beside its S/No (the box in the S/No heading ticks every item showing); items can also be ticked from the Tick items box under the filters (type to find). With items ticked, the summary cards (Closing Stock, Closing Value, Gross Profit, Reorder Alerts, or Stock Lines / Units / Stock Value / Low-Out on the other tabs), the Total row (it says how many items are ticked) and the export add up only those items; the table still lists everything, with ticked rows shaded, unless Show ticked only is switched on. Clear ticks goes back to all. Tabs: Stock Balance, Low and Out of Stock, Stock Movement, Stock Management (closing stock value; Total Value = sales incl. VAT; Cost Value = sold quantity x unit cost; Gross Profit = TV - CV; Profit % = gross profit / cost x 100; reorder alerts). At the end of Stock Management: a plain Total. Switch on "Credit notes & Net" (top right, off each time the page opens) to end instead with Gross (all items), Less credit notes (those dated in the period, for these items, at full value: TV, CV and profit) and Net (after credit notes); the Gross Profit card then shows the net. The export follows the switch. Stock Management has a Pricing filter (All pricing, NSPD, NSPH, NSPO, NSPV, No price code) where one or several lists can be ticked together (e.g. NSPH and NSPO): it shows only items on the ticked lists, and Sold, Total Value, Cost Value, Gross Profit and the credit notes count only sales made on those list codes, added together (opening, closing and cover stay the whole stock of the item). The search, category, store and pricing filters drive Gross, Less credit notes and Net too. Click any column heading to sort (again to reverse), e.g. Unit Cost, Total Value, Profit %. Export gives Excel/PDF/CSV, with those end rows. Stock Management columns: Opening, Received, Returns / Adj. (credit-note returns, returns to supplier, stock adjustments and counts: + in, - out), Sold, Closing; Opening + Received + Returns / Adj. Stock adjustment lines that show failed give the reason under the badge; pressing Apply to Inventory again retries them. An increase naming a batch number goes into that product own batch with that number (the same number on another product is never touched), or a new batch. - Sold = Closing, and Closing is the stock at the end of the "To" date (the Closing Stock card says the date). Where stock went: in Stock Management click a Sold figure to see each NSK invoice and customer that took it, with quantity and value (click the invoice or customer to open it; Export there too; "All movements of this item" opens its Stock Movement); click Received or Returns / Adj. to see those movements. Every row of Stock Balance, Low & Out of Stock and Stock Management has a History button (last column) that opens Stock Movement for just that item or size (this year so far, or the Stock Management period); "Show all items" clears it. Inventory Reports have no Item No. column (items go by description). Stock Movement shows one row per document per item or size (an order that took several batches, or was edited, is one row; the batches are listed): date, item, type, In/Out, Before/After (the stock of that item or size), and an Invoice / Document column showing just the invoice (or document); its arrow expands the row to show the flow in the direction the stock moved (out = Stock (batch) > Invoice > Customer, in = Supplier > Goods received > Stock (batch)) and the invoice details (invoice date, due date, amount, balance, status, order). Documents: (Invoice NSK/..., or INV-... while the invoice has no NSK yet; Credit note, Goods received, Return to supplier, Stock adjustment, Stock count, Opening stock - click to open it) and the customer or supplier (a customer opens their statement). Its filters: In & out / Out only (where it went) / In only (where it came from); the search also finds an invoice number or customer.'],
        ['CRM Reports > Customer Stats', '/reports/customers?tab=stats', ['can_view_reports_menu'],
            'Reports > CRM Reports (Customer Reports): two tabs. Customer Growth = new customers per day (chart and table). Customer Stats = every customer with S/No, Customer, Customer No., Phone, Orders, Order Value, Invoices, Invoiced, Paid, Balance, Last Order; customers with no orders show 0 and 0.00. Pick From/To dates to count only that period (blank = all time); Search by customer name, number or phone; Show all / with orders / without orders; click a column heading to sort (again to reverse); 20/50/250/All per page; Export gives Excel/PDF/CSV with totals.'],
        ['eTIMS (send an invoice to KRA)', '/etims', ['can_view_invoices', 'can_view_sales_menu'],
            'To send an invoice to KRA eTIMS: open the invoice (Sales > Invoices, or Filing) > its document > "eTIMS Invoice" (or eTIMS > Invoices, click the invoice > eTIMS invoice), then "Send to eTIMS". The page registers the items with KRA, adds any eTIMS stock the sale needs, submits it and updates by itself until KRA signs (usually under a minute). Then it shows KRA\'s own record laid out like KRA\'s eTIMS invoice: KRA logo, Invoice From/To PINs, CU invoice number (SCU ID/number) and date, every line under its KRA item code and tax rate, SCU information with KRA\'s QR code, and the tax summary (16%, 8%, 0%, Ex.); Print or Download PDF, and "View on KRA" opens KRA\'s receipt page. On eTIMS > Invoices, clicking a signed invoice also offers "Download eTIMS receipt", which saves that PDF straight away. If KRA refuses, the reason shows on the page with "Send to eTIMS" to try again. The buyer\'s KRA PIN goes to KRA only when it is valid (A or P, 9 digits, a letter, e.g. P051234567Z); a customer with "n/a", a phone number or a mistyped PIN (like PO51... with a letter O) is sent as a buyer without a PIN, name still printed - correct the PIN on the customer before sending if it should show. Sending never creates a product: each invoice line goes to KRA under the product it is for (an old line written for a product whose sizes became products of their own is linked to the right one by its name), and a line that matches no single product stops the invoice with "These lines are not linked to a product" - edit the invoice and pick the product. While eTIMS is on the Test environment it never gets in the way of anything: an invoice goes to KRA\'s sandbox only when someone presses Send to eTIMS (marked "Test - not a tax invoice"), nothing is sent by itself, and credit notes, deletions and order date changes go ahead whatever eTIMS says. eTIMS has been Live since 26/11/2026 (first live invoice NSK/0483), and even on Live nothing is raised by itself: an invoice goes to KRA only when someone clicks "Raise on eTIMS" on its page, and a credit note likewise (its own "Raise on eTIMS" once credited, and only after its invoice is KRA-signed); issuing a credit note never waits for eTIMS. Signed documents are locked. eTIMS > Product Codes lists items and their KRA codes (Export to Excel, fill in the Item Class Codes and import it back; the Ref column that ties each row to its product is hidden, so it is not edited, and a new product or a renamed one is matched too). Adding or editing a product has an optional "KRA Item Class Code" with a suggestion from its name ("Use"); left empty, the product goes to KRA under the general code 99010000 until one is set, and changing it registers a registered product again. A product\'s sizes share its KRA code. eTIMS stock follows the system: opening stock at registration, goods received (02), stock adjustments and counts (06 in / 16 out), breakages, damage and expiry (15) and returns to a supplier (12) are sent by themselves once Live, and a sale short of eTIMS stock adds the shortfall first - do not also add stock on the KRA portal; Settings > eTIMS (eTIMS Setup) holds the KRA PIN, branch, DigiTax API key, Test Connection and Enable.'],
        ['Procurement / CRM / Logistics Reports', '/reports', ['can_view_reports_menu'],
            'Reports menu: Procurement (purchases by supplier), CRM (customers), Logistics (deliveries), eTIMS Compliance (KRA submissions).'],
        ['HR & Payroll > Employees (add employee)', '/hr/employees', ['can_view_employees_menu'],
            'HR & Payroll > Employees: the staff list (Total/Active Employees at the top; search by name or email; Export for Excel/PDF/CSV). Click an employee to see their details. To add an employee: click "Add New Employee" and fill in Personal Information (Employee Number, Hire Date, First Name, Last Name, Email, Phone, Date of Birth, Gender, National ID), Address Information, Employment Information (Employment Type, Status, Department, Position, Supervisor, Leave Approver, Salary Advance Approver, and Connect User Account to link their login), Financial Information (Payment Frequency, Basic Salary, Bank Name/Account/Branch), Statutory Details (KRA PIN, NSSF Number, SHIF Number, disability exemption, insurance relief; on Edit Employee also "Statutory deductions apply" - off for someone paid a fixed amount with no PAYE/NSSF/SHIF/Housing Levy/NITA - and "SHIF exempt") and any allowances or deductions, then click "Save Employee". The new employee is then in the list and in payroll. Row menu: View, Edit Employee, Delete Employee; a leaver is set to Status Terminated with a Termination Date.'],
        ['HR & Payroll > Payroll', '/hr/payroll', ['can_view_payroll_menu'],
            'HR & Payroll > Payroll: the payroll runs, one per month, with S/No, Period, Status, Paid Date, Employees, Total Gross and Total Net. "New Run" picks a month and year (2000 onwards) - Create Draft, or Create & Process to work out every payslip from the employees\' salaries, allowances and today\'s statutory rates; a month before the current one leaves today\'s salary advances and one-time allowances for the current payroll. Statutory deductions follow the client\'s Payroll & KRA Compliance Tracker exactly, every amount rounded to whole shillings: NSSF 6% employee + 6% employer of pay up to 108,000 (Tier I first 9,000, Tier II the rest), SHIF 2.75% of gross (min 300), Housing Levy 1.5% + 1.5%, PAYE on gross less NSSF, SHIF and Housing Levy (bands 10% to 24,000, 25% to 32,333, 30% to 500,000, 32.5% to 800,000, 35% above; personal relief 2,400; insurance relief 15% up to 5,000), NITA KES 50 per employee (employer cost). On the employee: "Statutory deductions apply" off = paid a fixed amount with no deductions (e.g. Dr Wachira 42,500); "SHIF exempt" = no SHIF (e.g. Annah). A deduction named HELB is the HELB deduction (remitted to HELB); other deductions such as company loans stay as other deductions. November 2026: 11 people, gross 665,761, net 507,162, statutory 194,368 (PAYE 94,953, NSSF 60,840, SHIF 16,041, Housing Levy 18,698, HELB 3,336, NITA 500). STATUS FLOW: Draft (prepared and calculated) -> Approve (tick; management signs off - posts the payroll entry: DR Salaries + employer NSSF / Housing Levy / NITA, CR PAYE, NSSF, SHIF, Housing Levy, HELB, NITA and other deductions payable and Net Salaries Payable; no bank movement) -> Initiate payment (paper-plane icon: payment voucher with Paid from account, method, date, reference - still no bank entry) -> Confirm paid (green card icon: choose Paid from - the bank account or petty cash -, payment date, payment method and payment reference (required); only then does it post DR Net Salaries Payable / CR that account, and it shows in the bank book and Bank Reconciliation). Clicking Paid never assumes money left the bank; a payroll can be paid only once and a payment reference cannot be reused. The undo-arrow sends an approved, unpaid run back to Draft to correct it (its payroll entry is reversed). Payroll posts to the ledger from November 2026 on only; months before were booked as Salaries & Wages expenses, so from November salaries must not also be entered as expenses. The table shows Paid Date and Paid From (with the reference). TABS: Payroll Runs | Statutory Payments | Payment History. STATUTORY PAYMENTS tab: for each approved payroll month (from November 2026), what is owed to each body, filled from the payroll: PAYE (KRA iTax P10), NSSF (employee + employer), SHIF (SHA), Affordable Housing Levy (employee + employer), HELB (where applicable) and NITA (employer) - employee and employer shares in their own columns, total due, paid, balance, due date (9th of the next month; HELB the 15th) and status Pending / Partially Paid / Fully Paid (red "overdue" once the due date passes unpaid). "Pay" on a line: amount (up to the balance), payment date, Paid from account, method, payment reference (required) and an optional receipt / payment slip attachment and notes; confirming posts DR that payable / CR the bank (employer shares were expensed at approval). Part payments are allowed; the same reference cannot be used twice for an item and nothing can be paid beyond the balance. Click an item to see its payments (date, ref, account, receipt, who recorded it); the bin deletes a mistaken payment and reverses its entry. Each month card also shows the net salaries status. Year filter, totals and Export (Excel/PDF/CSV). PAYMENT HISTORY tab: every salary payment and statutory payment (audit trail) - payment date, payment, payroll month, paid to, paid from, method, reference, amount, recorded by - with From/To dates, total and Export. The arrow opens the run: summary cards (incl. HELB and NITA), each employee\'s payslip (View, Edit while draft), Export (Excel/PDF/CSV) and Export P10 for KRA. The bin icon deletes a payroll run in any status with its payslips (needs "Delete Payroll"); salary advances it deducted are owed again. PAYROLL HISTORY (every payroll since the company started, for the record and reconciliations): "History Template" downloads the Excel sheet - one row per employee per month with Month (03/2023 or March 2023), Employee No. or Employee Name, Basic Salary, Taxable / Non-Taxable Allowances, NSSF, NHIF / SHIF, Housing Levy, PAYE, Salary Advance, Other Deductions, Net Pay, Paid Date and Status (Paid/Approved/Draft, blank = Paid). "Import Payroll History" uploads it: Preview shows each row (created / updated / unchanged / problem) before Import saves anything. Each month becomes a run marked "Record", kept exactly as typed (Gross = Basic + Taxable Allowances; a typed Net Pay is kept even if the figures give a different amount, and the preview says so; the employer NSSF and Housing Levy shares are recorded equal to the employee\'s), and it never posts to the books, because those salaries are already there as expenses. Importing a month again updates its payslips; a month already run normally is left alone. Employees must exist in HR > Employees first (a leaver with their Termination Date). Record runs cannot be processed or edited payslip by payslip - change the sheet and import it again, or delete the run. The same sheet is also on Settings > Data Import.'],
        ['HR & Payroll > Leave Management', '/hr/leave-management', ['can_view_leave_management_menu'],
            'HR & Payroll > Leave Management: all leave requests. "Create Leave Request" records leave for an employee; open a request to approve or reject it. Employees apply for their own leave in the Employee Portal.'],
        ['HR & Payroll > Salary Advance', '/hr/salary-advance', ['can_view_salary_advance_menu'],
            'HR & Payroll > Salary Advance: all advance requests. "Create Salary Advance Request" for an employee; open one to approve or reject it. Employees request their own in the Employee Portal.'],
        ['HR & Payroll > Allowances', '/hr/allowances', ['can_view_employees_menu'],
            'HR & Payroll > Allowances: allowances paid to employees outside payroll, for the month picked: one-time ones and monthly ones set to "Disbursed" on the employee (Type column). "One-time Allowance" records one paid once: employee, allowance (e.g. Field allowance), amount, date paid, taxable, and - like an expense - the Account then the Sub-account it is charged to (e.g. Expenses > Salaries & Wages) and Paid from (Petty Cash, or Prime Bank by bank transfer or cheque); both are required. The list shows each allowance\'s account and paid-from. Payroll leaves it out; it shows in the month of its date and can be removed with its bin icon. "ROI for Reps" opens the report. Every allowance paid outside payroll is also an expense: each payment (a one-time allowance on its date, a monthly one on the 1st of each month or its start date) is recorded under Expenses, charged to the allowance\'s chosen sub-account and paid from its Petty Cash / Prime Bank (older allowances without a choice: the "Salaries & Allowances" category, cash), approved, with the employee as payee and "Allowance: <name>" as description, and posts to the books. It follows the allowance: changing the amount or dates updates those expenses, removing the allowance removes them, and each new month adds the monthly payment.'],
        ['Profit (report)', '/reports/profit', ['can_view_reports_menu'],
            'Reports > Profit: profit over any date range (Today, This week, This month or From/To). Total Value (TV) is each invoice line as billed, after discount, including VAT (a month\'s TV equals its Sales Accounts total; negotiated prices count as invoiced, e.g. NSPH 067 listed at 67 but sold at 45 counts 45). Cost Value (CV) is what the goods cost in the batches they left from on the order\'s dispatch; else the item\'s average batch cost; else its Cost Price (sales before 01/11/2026 never moved stock, so they always use these). Profit = TV - CV; Profit % = profit / CV x 100. A line with no cost recorded anywhere counts at cost 0 and is flagged ("No cost"); every such line is always listed in the amber "no cost recorded" panel above the table, grouped by item - expand an item to see its invoices (and credit notes) linked for corrections, type its Cost Price per piece and Save: the report reloads and the item leaves the list once costed (a line not linked to a product is fixed on its invoice). Three views: By Price List (NSPH, NSPO, NSPD, NSPV, "No price code"), By Product, Invoice Lines (NSK no., date, customer, item, price code, qty, per, rate, TV, CV, profit, profit %, cost from). A "Credit notes & Net" switch (off on every visit, as on Stock Management) adds Gross, Less credit notes (all credit notes dated in the range, at their full value as on Sales Accounts, minus the cost of the goods back) and Net (after credit notes) to the end of every view and Export, and the cards then show net; switched off, the table ends with Total and the cards show the figures before credit notes. Filters: price list, cost from, search; sortable; totals and Export (Excel/PDF/CSV) follow the filters.'],
        ['ROI for Reps (report)', '/reports/rep-roi', ['can_view_reports_menu'],
            'Reports > ROI for Reps: each sales rep\'s allowances (one-time on their date, monthly disbursed once a month) against the invoices raised under the rep (the invoice\'s sales rep, else its order\'s; net of credit notes; drafts and cancelled left out), over a date range (Range: Today, This week, This month, Last 90 days, or From/To for any day, week, month or range). ROI = invoiced / allowance (KES invoiced per KES 1 paid): green 10x and over, amber 1-10x, red under 1x. One table, one row per rep: click a column (Rep, Allowance, Invoices, Invoiced, ROI) to sort; filter by ROI band or search a rep; rows per page 20/50/250/All; totals and Export follow the filters. For a rep\'s invoices to count: their user\'s role must be a sales rep role, the order/invoice must name them as sales rep, and their employee record must be linked to that user (else the row says no user account linked).'],
        ['HR & Payroll > Time Management', '/hr/time-management', ['can_view_time_management_menu'],
            'HR & Payroll > Time Management: staff time entries. "Create Time Entry" to record one; row menu to view, edit or delete.'],
        ['HR & Payroll > Daily Reports (review staff daily work reports)', '/hr/daily-reports', ['can_view_daily_reports_menu'],
            'HR & Payroll > Daily Reports: the daily work reports staff submitted from the Employee Portal - Date, Employee, Position, Approver, Status (pending/approved/rejected). Filter by Date and by Status; row menu > View Details shows the Work / Activity Log, Key Achievements and Pending Work / Challenges.',
            ['can_approve_daily_reports' => 'Only Directors approve daily reports: every staff report, the GM\'s included, goes to the Directors (a Director\'s own report is approved automatically). A Director opens a pending report: row menu or View Details > Approve (optional Approval Remarks) or Reject (give the Rejection Reason). Anyone else sees "Awaiting approval by a Director" and no Approve/Reject.']],
        ['Employee Portal (my leave, salary advance, daily work report)', '/employee-portal', ['can_view_employee_portal_menu'],
            'Employee Portal: your own HR self-service. The top shows your Employment Summary (Employee Number, Department, Position, Basic Salary) and Approval Routing (Leave, Salary Advance and Daily Reports all go to any Director; a Director\'s own leave or advance goes to any other Director, and a Director\'s own daily report is self-certified). Tabs: Leave - "Apply for Leave" (see days left per leave type), then "Submit Leave Request"; Salary Advance - enter Amount, Request Date and Reason, then "Submit Salary Advance Request"; Daily Reports - the "Submit Daily Work Report" form is open right on the tab (there is no "New Report" button); to write today\'s report: pick the Date, fill the Work / Activity Log for each time slot, Today\'s Key Achievements and Pending Work / Challenges; what you type is saved automatically as you go ("Save Draft" saves it at once), and "Submit Daily Work Report" appears once every part is filled. Daily reports close at 12 midnight: anyone who has not sent the day\'s report gets a bell reminder at 8:00 PM and 10:00 PM, and at 12 midnight whatever they had written is sent to the Directors as it stands, marked "sent at midnight" (this runs on the server, so the page need not be open; Sundays and days on approved leave need no report). Your own reports are listed beside the form under Recent Daily Reports (filter by date) with their status; a pending one can be edited and resubmitted there. Daily reports go to the Directors, who approve or reject them on their own screen. Directors see a Pending Approvals panel here for leave, salary advances and daily reports. The GM and the Directors also see a Staff Activity panel: for any day, who has and has not sent their daily report (and who is still writing one), plus everyone\'s latest daily reports, leave requests and salary advances.'],
        ['Assets (asset register)', '/assets', ['can_view_assets_menu', 'can_view_assets'],
            'Assets (Fixed Asset Register, loaded 24/11/2026 from Nescopharm Fixed Asset Register.xlsx: 64 lines, 293 pieces, cost KES 7,105,598): each asset has an Asset ID by class (CMP- Computers & Laptops, PRN- Printers, FUR- Furniture & Fittings, OEQ- Office Equipment, MCH- Machinery & Equipment, MHE- Material Handling Equipment, SEC- Security Equipment, LHI- Leasehold Improvements), an Asset Tag No. (the label on it), description, class, serial/model, Location / Department, Assigned To / User (the person answerable for it), supplier, invoice no., quantity, purchase date, total cost (unit cost = total / qty) and Status (In Use, In Store, Under Repair, Missing, Disposed, Written Off). Depreciation is straight line by class: Computers 33.33% (3 yrs), Printers 25% (4), Furniture, Office Equipment and Machinery 12.5% (8), Material Handling and Security 20% (5), Leasehold Improvements 10% (10), nil residual; it starts on the later of the purchase date and 01/10/2025 (trading began), a full month charged in the start month, until the cost is written off. The page has an As at date: the cards (Assets, Total Cost, Accumulated Depreciation, Net Book Value, Not Assigned) and the table' + Q + 's Accum. Dep. and Net Book Value are as at that date; Summary by Asset Class shows lines, qty, rate, life, cost, annual and accumulated depreciation and NBV per class. As at 31/10/2026 accumulated depreciation was 1,042,902.53 and NBV 6,062,695.49. Filter by class, location, Assigned To (incl. Not assigned to anyone) and status; the Total row and the export follow the filters. Add Asset gives the next Asset ID in its class and fills the class rate and life; choosing a purchase date sets the depreciation start. Import reads the register workbook (its Register sheet) or the template: a row whose Asset ID is already there updates that asset, others are added, with a preview first. Export: Excel in the register' + Q + 's columns (imports back), CSV, or a PDF of who has what and its value. To retire an asset keep its record and set Status to Disposed or Written Off (NBV then 0). The page has two tabs: Register (all of the above) and Schedule - the register\'s Depreciation Schedule by Asset, a year-end projection: for every asset (S/No, Asset ID, Description, Class, Purchase Date, Total Cost, Dep. Start Date, Depreciable Amount, Monthly Depreciation, Life in months) three sections across the year-ends 31/12/2025, 31/12/2026 ... (6 by default; 3-12 can be picked): Accumulated depreciation at year-end, Depreciation charge for the year, Net book value at year-end; depreciation stops once an asset is fully depreciated; an asset bought later has nothing before its start. Filter by class, show one section or all three, TOTAL row (e.g. charge 2025: 240,458.30, 2026: 964,583.07; NBV 31/12/2026: 5,900,556.68 - the register sheet agrees to within cents) and Export (Excel/PDF/CSV).',
            ['can_create_assets|can_manage_assets' => 'To add one: "Add Asset", fill Asset Description (required), Asset Class (fills its rate and life), Tag No., Serial / Model No., Status, Location / Department, Assigned To / User, Supplier, Invoice No., Purchase Date, Quantity and Total Cost; Asset ID is given automatically; then save. Many at once: "Template" downloads the Excel sheet; fill it and click "Import", check the Import Preview (rows with errors are skipped), then confirm.',
             'can_update_assets|can_manage_assets' => 'Row menu > Edit changes an asset.',
             'can_delete_assets|can_manage_assets' => 'Row menu > Delete removes one.']],
        ['SOPs (procedures, temperature records, cleaning records)', '/sops', ['can_view_sops_menu', 'can_view_sops'],
            'SOPs has three tabs, each table with search/filters, sortable columns, paging and Export (Excel/PDF/CSV). SOPs: the procedures with year, status, annexures and comments; "New SOP" adds one. Temperature Records: "Log Today\'s Reading" per thermometer - morning reading before 12:00, afternoon from 12:00 (today only; any past date can be picked to type in an old chart), acceptance max (a reading above it is flagged Out of range). "Checked By" is picked from the people whose role has "Check SOP Records" (GM, Director and Accountant to start with) and they get a notification; "Recorded By" is whoever typed it in. Cleaning Records (the cleaning services sheet): "Record Cleaning" with date, start/end time, areas cleaned (e.g. All areas, Offices/windows, Holiday), cleaned by, and "Checked By" (required) - "Record & Send to Checker" notifies that person. The checker sees "Waiting for your check" at the top, clicks "Check", writes a comment and confirms; whoever prepared it is notified. Each record keeps who prepared it and who checked it. Past sheets for both tabs: "Template", fill it in Excel, "Import" (preview first) - a cleaning row with Checked By filled comes in already checked.',
            ['can_manage_company' => 'Only the GM or a Director can delete temperature or cleaning records, or edit a cleaning record after it has been checked.']],
        ['Users & Roles', '/users', ['can_manage_users_and_roles'],
            'Users: add a user with their email and role; they get an email with a link to set their password, which works for 24 hours (after that they use "Forgot password" on the login page). A role\'s permissions decide which menus and data they see. To change someone\'s role: on the Users tab open the row\'s Actions (...) menu and use "Assign Role" (or "Edit User" > Role). On the Roles tab a role\'s permissions are ticked on or off - e.g. "Check SOP Records" decides who can be picked as the checker of temperature and cleaning records. Anyone who forgot their password uses "Forgot password" on the login page: the emailed link sets a new one and signs out their old sessions. Capitals in an email address do not matter. System health: every 5 minutes the server checks the database, the API, the website, the AI assistant (Groq) and email. At 8:00am (Nairobi) a health summary is emailed with the last 24 hours; if anything goes down, at any hour (working hours 8:30-17:00 or after hours), an alert is emailed at once and repeated every 30 minutes until it is back, then a "back up" email. Recipients are set by the HEALTH_ALERT_EMAILS server setting or the "health_alert_emails" company setting.'],
    ];

    protected static function open(callable $can): \Illuminate\Support\Collection
    {
        return collect(self::AREAS)->filter(fn ($a) => collect($a[2])->contains(fn ($p) => $can($p)))->values();
    }

    /** Names and paths of the areas this user can open (the steps come from howTo, to keep prompts small). */
    public static function areaList(callable $can): string
    {
        return self::open($can)->map(fn ($a) => '[' . trim(preg_replace('/\s*\(.*\)$/', '', $a[0])) . "]({$a[1]})")->implode('; ');
    }

    /** An area's guide text for this user: the shared steps plus the ones their role allows. */
    protected static function guide(callable $can, array $area): string
    {
        $allowed = collect($area[4] ?? [])
            ->filter(fn ($text, $perms) => collect(explode('|', $perms))->contains(fn ($p) => $can($p)));
        $denied = count($area[4] ?? []) - $allowed->count();

        return trim($area[3] . ' ' . $allowed->implode(' '))
            . ($denied ? ' (This user\'s role cannot do the other actions here, such as adding, changing or approving; an administrator or approver does those.)' : '');
    }

    /** The guide text of the open areas that best match a question, e.g. "add product". */
    public static function howTo(callable $can, string $topic): array
    {
        $words = collect(preg_split('/\W+/', strtolower($topic)))->filter(fn ($w) => strlen($w) > 2)
            ->map(fn ($w) => rtrim($w, 's'));
        $scored = self::open($can)->map(function ($a) use ($words, $can) {
            $name = strtolower($a[0]);
            $text = strtolower($a[3] . ' ' . implode(' ', $a[4] ?? []));
            $score = $words->sum(fn ($w) => (str_contains($name, $w) ? 3 : 0) + (str_contains($text, $w) ? 1 : 0));
            // The screen's name without the hint in brackets: "[HR & Payroll > Employees](/hr/employees)".
            $label = trim(preg_replace('/\s*\(.*\)$/', '', $a[0]));
            return ['score' => $score, 'area' => $a[0], 'link' => "[{$label}]({$a[1]})", 'how' => self::guide($can, $a)];
        })->filter(fn ($a) => $a['score'] > 0)->sortByDesc('score')->take(3)->values();

        return $scored->isEmpty()
            ? ['note' => 'No matching area the user can open. Areas: ' . self::areaList($can)]
            : ['areas' => $scored->map(fn ($a) => collect($a)->except('score'))->all()];
    }

    /** The path of the screen a text names ("Inventory Reports > Stock Balance" is /reports/inventory), the most specific name first. */
    public static function pathFor(string $text): ?string
    {
        $text = strtolower(str_replace(['→', '›'], '>', $text));
        return collect(self::AREAS)
            ->map(fn ($a) => ['label' => strtolower(trim(preg_replace('/\s*\(.*\)$/', '', $a[0]))), 'path' => $a[1]])
            ->filter(fn ($a) => str_contains($text, $a['label']))
            ->sortByDesc(fn ($a) => strlen($a['label']))
            ->first()['path'] ?? null;
    }

    /** Area names this user cannot open, so the assistant can say so instead of guessing. */
    public static function closedAreas(callable $can): array
    {
        return collect(self::AREAS)
            ->reject(fn ($a) => collect($a[2])->contains(fn ($p) => $can($p)))
            ->map(fn ($a) => trim(preg_replace('/\s*\(.*\)$/', '', $a[0])))->all();
    }
}
